Simulating the effects of epigenetic changes on adaptive evolution in a population using Java code would require a complex program that models the various factors involved. However, I can provide you with an example of a Java program that models the impact of epigenetic modifications on the expression of genes and how these changes can affect the fitness of individuals in a population over time.
The following is an example of a Java program that models the impact of epigenetic modifications on gene expression and fitness in a population:
```
import java.util.Random;
public class EpigeneticsSimulation {
    public static final int POPULATION_SIZE = 1000;
    public static final int GENERATIONS = 100;
    public static final double MUTATION_RATE = 0.01;
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Initialize population
        Individual[] population = new Individual[POPULATION_SIZE];
        for (int i = 0; i < POPULATION_SIZE; i++) {
            population[i] = new Individual();
        }
        // Run simulation for specified number of generations
        for (int generation = 0; generation < GENERATIONS; generation++) {
            // Calculate fitness for each individual in the population
            for (Individual individual : population) {
                individual.calculateFitness();
            }
            // Create a new population by selecting individuals with high fitness
            Individual[] newPopulation = new Individual[POPULATION_SIZE];
            for (int i = 0; i < POPULATION_SIZE; i++) {
                Individual parent1 = selectParent(population);
                Individual parent2 = selectParent(population);
                Individual child = parent1.mateWith(parent2);
                child.mutate(MUTATION_RATE);
                newPopulation[i] = child;
            }
            // Replace old population with new population
            population = newPopulation;
        }
    }
    // Select a parent from the population based on fitness
    private static Individual selectParent(Individual[] population) {
        Random random = new Random();
        double totalFitness = 0;
        for (Individual individual : population) {
            totalFitness += individual.getFitness();
        }
        double randomFitness = random.nextDouble() * totalFitness;
        double currentFitness = 0;
        for (Individual individual : population) {
            currentFitness += individual.getFitness();
            if (currentFitness >= randomFitness) {
                return individual;
            }
        }
        return null;
    }
}
class Individual {
    private static final int NUM_GENES = 100;
    private static final double EPIGENETIC_RATE = 0.1;
    private static final double FITNESS_FACTOR = 0.01;
    private boolean[] genes;
    private double fitness;
    public Individual() {
        Random random = new Random();
        genes = new boolean[NUM_GENES];
        for (int i = 0; i < NUM_GENES; i++) {
            genes[i] = random.nextBoolean();
        }
    }
    public void calculateFitness() {
        double epigeneticFactor = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < NUM_GENES; i++) {
            if (genes[i]) {
                epigeneticFactor *= (1 + EPIGENETIC_RATE);
            } else {
                epigeneticFactor *= (1 - EPIGENETIC_RATE);
            }
        }
        fitness = epigeneticFactor * FITNESS_FACTOR;
    }
    public Individual mateWith(Individual other) {
        Random random = new Random();
        Individual child = new Individual();
        for (int i = 0; i < NUM_GENES; i++) {
            if (random.nextBoolean()) {
                child.genes[i] = genes[i];
            } else {
                child.genes[i] = other.genes[i];
            }
        }
        return child;
    }
    public void mutate(double mutationRate) {
        Random random = new Random();
        for (int i = 0; i < NUM_GENES; i++) {
            if (random.nextDouble() < mutationRate) {
                genes[i] = !genes[i];
            }
        }
    }
    public double getFitness() {
        return fitness;
    }
}
```
In this program, we create a population of individuals with a set number of genes (100 in this case). Each gene can be either "on" or "off", and the epigenetic factor of an individual is calculated based on the state of their genes. The fitness of an individual is then calculated based on their epigenetic factor and a fitness factor.
The program then runs for a specified number of generations, during which we select parents from the population based on fitness and create a new population by mating these parents and introducing mutations in their offspring. The fitness of each individual is then recalculated based on their new genes and epigenetic factor.
This program is a simple example of how epigenetic changes can affect the fitness of individuals in a population over time. However, simulating the effects of epigenetic changes on adaptive evolution in a population is a complex process that requires a more sophisticated model.

