```swift
func minimumWheels(_ lawn: [[Int]], _ k: Int) -> Int {
    let n = lawn.count
    let m = lawn[0].count
    var wheels = 0
    var visited = Array(repeating: Array(repeating: false, count: m), count: n)

    func dfs(_ i: Int, _ j: Int) {
        visited[i][j] = true
        for di in -k...k {
            for dj in -k...k {
                let ni = i + di
                let nj = j + dj
                if ni >= 0 && ni < n && nj >= 0 && nj < m && !visited[ni][nj] && lawn[ni][nj] == 1 {
                    dfs(ni, nj)
                }
            }
        }
    }

    for i in 0..<n {
        for j in 0..<m {
            if lawn[i][j] == 1 && !visited[i][j] {
                dfs(i, j)
                wheels += 1
            }
        }
    }

    return wheels
}
```
The function `minimumWheels` takes two parameters: a 2D array of integers `lawn` representing the lawn, and an integer `k` representing the coverage of each wheel. The function returns an integer representing the minimum number of wheels required to cover the entire lawn.
The function first initializes some variables: `n` and `m` are the dimensions of the lawn, `wheels` is the number of wheels needed, and `visited` is a 2D array of booleans representing whether each section of the lawn has been visited during the DFS traversal.
The function then defines a nested function `dfs` that performs a DFS traversal starting from a given position `(i, j)` in the lawn. The traversal marks each visited section as visited and recursively visits all adjacent garden sections within the coverage of the wheel.
The function then loops over all sections of the lawn and calls `dfs` on each unvisited garden section. Each call to `dfs` increments the `wheels` counter.
Finally, the function returns the `wheels` counter.
Note that this solution assumes that the input lawn is a valid rectangular grid of integers, where 0 represents a path and 1 represents a garden section. If the input is not valid, the function may produce unexpected results or crash.

