```java
// Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) - Separate interfaces for different clients
interface DataAccess {
  void connect();
  void disconnect();
  void execute(String query);
}
interface ReportGenerator {
  void generateReport();
}
// Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) - High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules
class ReportService {
  private DataAccess dataAccess;
  private ReportGenerator reportGenerator;
  public ReportService(DataAccess dataAccess, ReportGenerator reportGenerator) {
    this.dataAccess = dataAccess;
    this.reportGenerator = reportGenerator;
  }
  public void generateReport() {
    dataAccess.connect();
    reportGenerator.generateReport();
    dataAccess.disconnect();
  }
}
class MySQLDataAccess implements DataAccess {
  public void connect() {
    // Connect to MySQL database
  }
  public void disconnect() {
    // Disconnect from MySQL database
  }
  public void execute(String query) {
    // Execute query on MySQL database
  }
}
class PDFReportGenerator implements ReportGenerator {
  public void generateReport() {
    // Generate PDF report
  }
}
public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    DataAccess dataAccess = new MySQLDataAccess();
    ReportGenerator reportGenerator = new PDFReportGenerator();
    ReportService reportService = new ReportService(dataAccess, reportGenerator);
    reportService.generateReport();
  }
}
```
In this code, the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) is applied by separating the `DataAccess` and `ReportGenerator` interfaces for different clients. This allows clients to use only the methods they need, which reduces unnecessary dependencies and improves maintainability.
The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) is applied by using the `ReportService` class to decouple the high-level `generateReport` method from the low-level `DataAccess` and `ReportGenerator` implementations. This allows for easier testing and swapping of implementations, which improves maintainability and portability.
The Dependency Injection design pattern is used to inject the dependencies into the `ReportService` class via its constructor. This allows for better control and flexibility over the dependencies, which improves maintainability and portability.
Overall, applying these design principles and patterns enhances software quality, maintainability, and portability by reducing dependencies, decoupling high-level and low-level modules, and allowing for easier testing and swapping of implementations. This also helps with cross-platform compatibility as the code can be easily adapted to different platforms with different implementations of the `DataAccess` and `ReportGenerator` interfaces.

